.TH RNGDUMP 1
.SH NAME
rngdump \- the hardware random number generator's own bytes, for assessment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rngdump
[
.B -a
] [
.B -n
.I bytes
]
.I file
.br
.B rngdump
.B -b
[
.B -a
]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Rngdump
writes bytes of the native kernel's hardware generator, as the board's
driver gives them and before any deterministic generator, to
.IR file ,
for SP 800-90B's entropy assessment.
It reads
.BR /dev/hwrandom ,
.I bytes
of it (default 1000000), or with
.BR -b ,
.BR /dev/hwrandomboot ,
the first 4096 bytes the generator gave on this boot.
.B -a
appends to
.I file
rather than replacing it.
.PP
When done it prints one line of
.IB key = value
pairs:
.BR src ,
.BR bytes ,
.B ms
and
.B rate
(bytes a second),
.B sha256
of what was written,
.B mcv
(the most common byte's count),
.B chisq
(chi-square of the byte counts, 255 degrees of freedom),
.BR bit0 " to " bit7
(ones in each bit position, per million) and
.B lag1
(bytes equal to the one before, per million).
They show quickly a source that is plainly broken; the estimate is made on
the host by
.BR tools/entropy/assess.py .
.SH FILES
.TF /dev/hwrandomboot
.TP
.B /dev/hwrandom
the generator's bytes, read as fast as it gives them
.TP
.B /dev/hwrandomboot
this boot's first 4096
.PD
.PP
Both are given only to eve, and only on a boot whose command line holds the
word
.BR rngassess ;
on any other boot they refuse to open.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/rngdump.b
.br
.B /os/port/random.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3),
.B docs/ENTROPY-ASSESSMENT.md
